e but  unlike the loose center at the met which  is just encased in captivity in plexi  this is all  you can call things and bring them out  for a particular class so with denise  leite who's the curator of asian art  we ran a joint class on on lacquer in a  global context not just east asian  lacquer but also japaning from europe  and america  to talk about  non-bond  export ware from japan  but handling things looking at things  asking questions of the objects as we  looked inside of them was a critical  part of it  i've also tried to  sort of follow the principles of john  dewey learning by doing  um and  what i tried to do is sort of put making  exercises into various different courses  initially in the old furniture study  they had sort of a shop and i would  bring in um a friend of mine peter  fallinsby who used to be the joiner at  plymouth plantation an open air museum  up in massachusetts and we  worked out sort of different exercises  we could do within two hours to give  students a chance to split out green  wood to plane it to learn how to do some  preliminary carving not to make  something in its entirety but just get a  few principles  this is something that i've done carried  forward as another part of west campus  scott strobel when he was head of west  campus gave me a space out there  to use as a low-tech maker space that i  could store some tools some materials  out there  and i started to incorporating this so  that's where i've done uh woodworking  now on the left for uh first year  seminar in 2019  i had another one  class on vessels in which we  won  class we ended up hammering copper disks  into bowls to learn something about um  hammering uh the malleability of metal  this is um  again sort of a green woodworking i did  for this material literacy and then  embodied artisanal knowledge using just  basic tools like clubs froze  hatchets  and knives  and  sending people to work the advantage of  fall in new england  you can work outside so  one year we were carving spoons uh on  west campus around the fire pit um it  wasn't available this year  so we went to one of the courtyards of  west campus and the collections study  center  with a shaving horse  again hatchets froze splitting out wood  green moist wood and then  working on different spatulas and  the like  all this is  working closely with different kinds of  artisans um so peter fallinsby um andre  scar says we're helpful with the wood  joan parcher a metal smith up in  providence  i've worked with her to developing a  very basic kit of tools that i could  give students to work with  making copper discs learning how to  shape something  with a ball-peen hammer  on a  on a steel block  i worked with andrew hamilton  developing another session on spinning  and fiber  and watching students get frustrated  trying to use a drop spindle um  you know these very basic uh sorts of  endeavors but you learn so much about  the materials and the feedback on them  that you can just see um the ways in  which when you have them uh students in  class the way they get absorbed time  flies  for them  i've also sent out kits ceramic kits  metal kits  and weaving kits to students for the  undergraduate class that they do  in their own dorms this was a covid  related sort of endeavor and again  students just raved about do learning  off-screen  and being able to sort of  learn so much and then apply what  they've learned in the grappling with  the material to their own work  again with andrew we did some weaving  this is some of the cardboard loom  weaving we did for the undergraduate  just  providing them with uh cotton warp  thread warp cardboard that sets up at  the loom and then a series of acrylics  that are sort of wool-like  so this is two different students work  just completed this year  i've worked with mark potter to work on  sort of  different sorts of recipes for clay  that you know you can add different  elements to it you can create different  sorts of appearances  we did this exercise in which we gave  students an hour in which they worked  with  prescribed  proportion recipes to make something and  then we gave them unlimited materials  and had them  measure out and these were the measuring  devices they had just a piece of wood  with holes drilled in it so they had to  work out proportions in their minds  of what these materials were going to do  this is the kit of tools we used to then  work with either regular clay  mixed or we could start add in different  kinds of ingredients that made their own  recipe  once they made them  we made them making test strips to see  you know hear these different uh  formulas  when they fired some of them didn't do  so well they shrunk they  bent around some of them were not very  stable and some of them were disasters  i feel like some of the students didn't  know when you when you're baking for  instance you do dry ingredients first  and then add liquid there were some  people who did liquid first  and just peeled all the we were working  on sheetrock and it just like made a  mess on the boards that they were  working on you learn a lot along the way  and then these are just some of the  objects i had everybody do a test strip  and an object  with a  given formula that they had to mix and  then also with their own invention  what i've done recently for the  undergraduates is working with penelope  von grinsvin who's  taken on responsibility of the five  undergraduate pottery studios is to  develop a kit that you could coil and  this um so it has a folding board with  canvas a cutting wire  two and a half pounds three pounds of  clay a bucket with a sponge you know um  instead of a needle tool a rib  everything you need to make  objects like this these are not thrown  on a wheel these are all coiled or slab  construction these are undergraduates  this past fall  um this semester who are making these  sorts of things with this kind of  exercise just giving clay abandoning  wheel to sort of like a lazy susan just  to get some sort of sense of what's  possible  so  you know in teaching material literacy  yale's got this incredible opportunity  in terms of access to collections things  that will inspire you  ways to then try out making yourself  exposed to different sorts of materials  and then also taking that knowledge back  to the classroom looking handling at  objects not simply looking at them under  a vitrine and it's just a couple things  that are important to this idea of the  pedagogy of teaching  object-driven inquiry  is in addition to having objects study  classrooms in abundance and it's  wonderful to see the peabody with their  renovations doing the same thing  but to  also start to realize well if you can  have a messy space can you design  um can you sort of work with  sympathetic knowledgeable makers nobody  one person can do it all so oftentimes  it's a collaboration um like to and  throwing with joan parcher what would be  a good exercise what i tried the first  time didn't work  and that's when i got her into the  conversation  these are things that you know i was  never trained in it but it's wonderful  to sort of develop these ideas together  and then i think the other thing that's  important is thinking about time  in terms of what's what can you  accomplish within two hours  what's a project and so many yale  students are  perfectionists who have an idea of what  they want to make and you have to get  them to shift that aside and think just  go with a process don't worry about  what you want to exert your will on  something what can you learn  along the way so it's been a fabulous  kind of  path to to go down to thinking about the  material humanities in some respects  into thinking about what yale really  allows us to do  and i hope that you get a chance to sort  of visit with some of the other  information tables  from  the  just thinking of  the wordle center in the art gallery  study centers on west campus of the ipch  institute for preservation of cultural  heritage and the research scientists  there conservation  staff for the various museums it's this  kind of package that you've got so many  people you can turn to  to ask questions  and to work together to sort of move  some of this project forward  so i'm happy to answer questions this i  just want to sort of  tempt you in terms of what's possible  [Applause]  i'm happy to start off so  when we were talking about the digital  humanities the question of sort of the  skills the coding skills and whatnot  that  are needed are not sort of part of the  curriculum that we understand are there  ways in which you can see this sort of  work being incorporated into curriculum  or how you've  yeah i mean what i've managed to do the  question is um how to  integrate this into a curriculum and  that's why i wanted to actually show  what i've been doing in some of the  classes  so with the undergraduates i haven't  necessarily  other than the furniture class i haven't  taught them in class time itself  but i developed these kits last year  when people were remote um that actually  had them pick up or mailed to them if  they were truly off campus  and they would make things photograph  them and then ship the kit well the  weaving kit was inexpensive so that was  just a throwaway but the um the ceramic  kit and the metal kit ended up  investing 25 to 35 dollars for each of  those kits  so  that's a way in which  i felt like that became an assignment  you know making and reflecting and  really giving them parameters in which  they could succeed  and they had to write reflections as i  said so they're they're it's not just  simply  winging it and then you know that's the  end of it but by reflecting  and giving them prompting certain  questions was a way to  dig deeper um into what this meant in  terms of the course for the graduate  students um  i do it within class because you've got  a longer period of time um and and i  will try to develop say three or four  making exercises over the semester um so  this course i'm teaching now on embodied  knowledge  we did woodworking first and that the  emphasis there was on the materials what  does the material what's the agency of  the material because it's green wood and  it's it's going to go the way it wants  to go um so you have to work with it and  then when we did metal um it was about  um thinking about the body because i had  them working at a bench on the ground  sitting all sorts just kept moving their  body positions around to get them  finding what's the right way and again  they had to write reflections and then  we just did pottery on  on wednesday and  there i was trying to get them to focus  on the hand and again having them on the  ground at a wheel coiling  all sorts of different movements so  there's a way to do it to incorporate it  and then there's also like the  undergraduates have this opportunity  in the residential colleges that there  are a number of different studios like  five pottery studios there's a weaving  studio in morrison styles  but it's harder for the graduate  students to gain access to that  but it's not you know i'm not running an  art school course either um so you know  it's that fine line um it's gotta tie  into  the theme the reading the intellectual  content uh of the course  but i was also wondering if you teach  this  to faculty  i  have there was  there have been a couple times that  i had the opportunity to have  a teach-in  on west campus  that was designed for graduate students  faculty and museum people  and i had different themes and brought  different people into run  different sorts of projects so  one was on this idea of  [Music]  spoons i mean we sort of have a session  which we had  makers artists talk about their practice  so that one day a second day would be  making things on west campus and the  third day  would be about  exhibition practice  so there was one year we did one uh that  had sort of uh the idea of  reproducibility that we had spoons we  cast spoons we carved spoons we made  clay spoons we hammered copper spoons  and just kept rotating people around  always working with the same spoon and  yet also had  andres garces who was working  in conservation at that point um he had  scanned one of these spoons and then  we did 3d printing  as well as cnc  subtractive technology and wood and  started working off of  what's the difference between a cast  pewter spoon and a  cnc  cut out spoon or a  3d printed spoon um and it was great  conversations just to sort of explode  that there was another year we talked  about um  the idea of uh of repair um and um  in in different kinds of uh  it's a recalibration that we had  christina kim who's a textile artist  come in and talk about in a sewing  circle um we had sam thomas uh  hotness sony beater who came in and  led a beating exercise to talk about  reconciliation that he's been doing with  the  canadian boarding schools  indigenous people  and was just incredible to think about  reconciliation repair um and how  making and materials really lend  themselves to this easygoing  conversation  so that's that's been one thing um  it's hard to do it every year um instead  of just coming up with these ideas  trying to mobilize people i think in  terms of stories um anecdotes of um  it's  i mean what what i've really been struck  by with a graduate seminar than when  we're working with different materials  watching people all of a sudden just  relax  and start to  realize  i don't have to be a perfectionist i'm  just i'm going with the flow the idea of  flow and you know  one of the topics that's come up this  year is what does focus mean what is  concentration what is absorption um and  how can you be focused in an area  where you're all hammering and all  sudden like that  that white noise starts to become a way  that you create your own little bubble  versus  being outdoors on a beautiful fall day  and you're just using a simple little  sloyd knife doing your final cuts on a  spatula and you're just you know  you're completely removed from the world  um so it's it's it's interesting to just  sort of watching people's bodies relax  watching  when they're social with one another  when they're sort of looking uh at their  own work and you start to see that how  it affects the way in which they're  talking about objects uh in subsequent  classes i don't know if that answers  some of your question  thanks cindy thank you  [Applause]  [Music]  you
 

